r than duplicated is the measurement: `totalNonDocMutatedLinesWithMeasure` and `deriveSessionKey` are imported from the nudge, so the two sides cannot drift on what a non-doc mutated line is or on how a session is keyed.", "per_event_budget_impact": "+1 on the session_start slot of ONE platform. Emission is zero bytes — the concern writes no stdout at all, so no per-concern row is registered in hook-token-budget.json and it cannot contribute to the per-slot sum. The cost is wall-clock rather than tokens, and it is stated rather than estimated: measured on this tree, `git rev-parse HEAD` 11-14 ms, `git diff --numstat HEAD` 22-29 ms, `git ls-files --others` 55-57 ms — about 91 ms, ONCE per session, behind an `existsSync` latch that makes a second session_start a no-op. On the reader's side the change is net-negative on two of three branches: `currentHeadSha` is now passed as a thunk, so the `absent` and `unreadable` paths — the steady state on every host without the nudge — no longer spawn a git process they would have discarded.", "reason": "`end_review_nudge_hook`'s own header recorded a measurement defect and the reason it was not fixed: pre-existing uncommitted changes are charged to the session, because 'no such baseline exists anywhere a `stop` concern can read it (recording one would be new session-wide state, out of this roadmap phase's one-concern scope)'. That reason went stale — session_start is bound on seven hosts carrying fourteen concerns — and the defect escalated: on an accumulator branch the nudge read 1,771 lines of two crashed sessions' uncommitted work and fired every turn. An advisory that always fires is noise, and noise is worse than silence because it trains the reader to skip the one that mattered. Three states make the subtraction invalid — no baseline, an unreadable one, a moved HEAD, and a mixed measure — and all of them fall back to the UNSUBTRACTED count rather than to silence, because a safety advisory must not be disarmed by its own instrumentation failing. Each fallback is carried into the `review_skipped` row as a closed enum, so the rate at which the mechanism degrades is measurable from its own stream rather than assumed. Honest limit, recorded here and at the mechanism: the baseline is written once and never refreshed, so from a session's first commit onward `head_moved` holds and the pre-existing tree is charged again. Re-baselining is not attempted because a second write cannot tell 'we committed our own work' from 'a peer session committed underneath us' from a stop payload."}
